  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs) are considered equivalent, effective first-line treatments for high blood pressure, though large observational studies show that ARBs offer similar cardiovascular protection with a lower rate of side effects.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/high-blood-pressure/in-depth/ace-inhibitors/art-20047480"
          },
          {
            "text": "Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs)",
            "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4303500/"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs) are considered equivalent, effective first-line treatments for high blood pressure"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Effectiveness: Both drug classes lower blood pressure and reduce the risk of major cardiovascular events (like heart attacks and strokes) just as effectively .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism of Action:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors (ending in -pril, e.g., lisinopril) stop the body from producing angiotensin II, a chemical that narrows blood vessels. They also break down bradykinin."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs (ending in -sartan, e.g., losartan) block angiotensin II from attaching to receptors on blood vessels, leading to relaxation without affecting bradykinin breakdown ."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects & Tolerability:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors: Frequently cause a persistent, dry cough (in 3% to 4% of patients) and carry a risk of angioedema (swelling of the face, lips, or tongue), which is more common in individuals of African descent."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs: Rarely cause a dry cough or angioedema, making them a preferred alternative for people who cannot tolerate ACE inhibitors."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              6,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Side Effects: Both classes can cause elevated potassium levels ( ), dizziness or fatigue from low blood pressure ( ), and temporary changes in kidney function.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtjIUnpKUI0"
              },
              {
                "link": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T_pY6qN02Pk"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Contraindications: Both drug classes are strictly unsafe during pregnancy (teratogenic) and should be avoided or closely monitored in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis . They should never be combined with each other due to a higher risk of kidney injury and low blood pressure without extra benefit."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Historical Standard: ACE inhibitors have a longer clinical history and older trial data, which is why historical medical guidelines frequently recommended them first .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              10
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Current Shift: Modern data\u2014including a massive international study of over 3 million patients published via and the American Heart Association\u2014support initiating treatment with ARBs because of their superior tolerability profile.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.tctmd.com/news/arbs-preferable-ace-inhibitors-large-observational-hypertension-study"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          5,
          6,
          8,
          9,
          0,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you or someone you are asking for is choosing a medication, sharing current kidney function, history of a cough/angioedema, or other health conditions (like diabetes or heart failure) can help clarify which option fits best."
      }
    ],
